#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""soak — run one nextest filter N times under the run wrapper, tabulate
verdict, wall and load per run.

The repeat-runner: three flight 13 beads each ask "how often, over N runs, at
what load" and the tree held no instrument that could answer — suite-profile
reads one junit report after the fact and cannot select an arm or repeat it.

    soak -E '<filter>' --runs N [--threads T] [--profile P]
    soak --self-test

Each iteration runs `cd fleet && tools/dolt-test-server cargo nextest run
--workspace -E '<filter>'` once, under the same TT_FLEET_TEST_OK=1 carve-out
the Makefile's fleet-test recipe requires (Alberto's ruling
2026-09-14) — soak bypasses that recipe (no fmt/anchors/clippy, no budget
verdict) but not its gate, because it is still a measurement run of the same
disabled suite. Before each iteration the box's 1-minute load is read the way
the fleet-test recipe's before-line does (uptime), pasted into that run's row.

VERDICT COMES FROM NEXTEST'S OWN PER-TEST STATUS TOKEN, never from counting
"failed" in the Summary line: a TIMEOUT test's Summary reads "...1 timed
out..." and never contains the word "failed", and a LEAK test's Summary reads
"...1 passed (1 leaky)...", also with no "failed" — a grep for FAIL alone
reports both as clean (a captured run already showed exactly this shape; the
fixtures self-test reproduces both verbatim, captured bytes from a real run).
The five tokens nextest prints, one per test, ANSI-coloured, of the shape
`    <TOKEN> [   0.012s] (1/1) <binary> <test name>`: PASS, FAIL, TIMEOUT,
LEAK, SIGKILL. nextest-version is pinned at fleet/.config/nextest.toml to
0.9.111; the token shapes here are read from that release's console output.

A run whose filter selects nothing, or whose output carries no parseable
Summary at all (a build or config failure before nextest starts), is nextest
ITSELF failing to run — never tabulated as a verdict. soak stops at the first
one of those and exits 2 naming it, rather than repeating a command that
cannot answer the question N times.

A run whose verdict is not PASS NAMES THE ARM that carried it, in the table's
last column and in the JSON row: a table of counts cannot be read against an
arm, and the filter that produced the run selects more arms than the one that
broke. Several arms at the same worst verdict render as the first plus a
`+N more` count, and the JSON row's `arms` carries all of them.

Exit codes: 0 every run PASS; 1 at least one run's verdict was FAIL, TIMEOUT,
LEAK or SIGKILL (a leak is not a clean pass — soak exists partly to catch one);
2 nextest itself could not run (no match, or no Summary parsed), or --self-test
disagreeing. Table then one JSON line, so a delivery note can quote the table
and a later tool can read the JSON.

Conventions: brain/harness/tools.md.
"""

